<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uesugi Kagekatsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1556">1556</a>–<a href="/years/1623">1623</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uesugi Kagekatsu was the adopted heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, who won the succession war after Kenshin's death in 1578. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> he was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> with large holdings. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1600, and after the Tokugawa victory his lands were cut to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a> Domain, which his descendants ruled until 1871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu.jpg" alt="Uesugi Kagekatsu" width="320" height="775" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Uesugi_Kagekatsu_Portrait_Yonezawa_City_Uesugi_Museum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
